# zenresize per-kernel NEON-vs-scalar — u8_to_f32_row was 1.67x SLOWER; fixed
date:  2026-07-28
host:  Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
build: release, NO -C target-cpu=native
bench: cargo bench --bench kernel_tiers   (1920-px RGBA rows)

## Why per-kernel

zenresize had only ever been measured END-TO-END (whole-image resize: 1.56x
sRGB / 4.29x linear on aarch64). An end-to-end number cannot reveal a single
kernel that is SLOWER than its own scalar fallback — the faster kernels hide it.
That failure mode turned up in garb, zensim, zentone and zenpng during the same
sweep (in zenpng, six of eight filter paths were losing), so it was worth
checking here too.

## Result

| kernel                   | NEON    | scalar  | ratio |
|--------------------------|---------|---------|-------|
| u8_to_f32_row            | 2.00us  | 1.20us  | 0.60x |  <- SLOWER
| f32_to_u8_row            | 2.60us  | 2.60us  | 1.00x |
| premultiply_alpha_row    | 2.00us  | 2.00us  | 1.00x |
| unpremultiply_alpha_row  | 2.90us  | 2.90us  | 1.00x |
| premultiply_u8_row       | 1.10us  | 1.10us  | 1.00x |
| unpremultiply_u8_row     | 8.40us  | 8.50us  | 1.01x |

One loser. The 1.00x rows are NOT gaps — on AArch64 NEON is baseline, so the
forced-scalar arm autovectorises too and 1.00x means the arms compile to
equivalent code (the lesson recorded in zentone's aces_ap1 write-up).

## Cause and fix

`u8_to_f32_row_impl` widens four pixels with
`i32x4::from_array(token, [inp[0] as i32, inp[1] as i32, ...])` — scalar lane
assembly feeding a vector convert. The autovectoriser handles the plain
`*i as f32 * (1.0/255.0)` loop better than that hand-built widening.

aarch64 now takes `u8_to_f32_row_scalar`:

  2.00us -> 1.30us    1.54x

Bit-identical — verified 0 differing lanes at n = 7680/1023/17/4/3/1 (including
lengths below one f32x4 and a single element). 249 tests green.

## Note

`u8_to_f32_row_impl` is declared `#[magetypes(neon, wasm128)]` — it has no
scalar tier of its own, so the incant! fallback reaches a separately written
`simd/scalar.rs::u8_to_f32_row_scalar`. That is the function dispatched to here.
